 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "# Dakota1890: the chain, end to end\n\nOne 1890 dictionary becomes a verifiable training environment. This notebook walks the whole chain that [HarleyCoops/Dakota1890](https://github.com/HarleyCoops/Dakota1890) built around it, and ends by running the published RL adapter and scoring what it writes.\n\n```\nRiggs 1890 (public-domain scans)\n      |  VLM extraction (separate, not in this notebook)\n      v\nlexicon graph  ->  verifiable tasks  ->  deterministic verifiers (the reward)\n      |                                     |\n      v                                     v\nStage 1: supervised warm start (Tinker)  Stage 2: GRPO RL (Tinker)\n      |                                     |\n      +---------------> published adapter <-+\n                              |\n                              v\n                  this notebook: sample inference, scored live\n```\n\n| Section | Needs | Costs |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Setup and task data | nothing | free |\n| 2. The verifiers (the reward function) | nothing | free |\n| 3. Optional: a 1-step RL smoke on Tinker | `TINKER_API_KEY` | < $0.05 |\n| 4. Inference with the published adapter, scored live | a GPU runtime (T4 is enough) | free |\n\nEverything here is CPU-only except section 4. The full training pipeline and its results are documented in the [repo README](https://github.com/HarleyCoops/Dakota1890)."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "# 0. Setup. transformers >= 5 is what the repo's runs use; peft loads the published adapter.\n%pip install -q \"transformers>=5.0\" peft accelerate datasets huggingface_hub"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "import os, subprocess, sys\n\n# Shallow clone, no LFS blobs (we do not need the scanned PDF here).\nenv = dict(os.environ, GIT_LFS_SKIP_SMUDGE=\"1\")\nif not os.path.exists(\"Dakota1890\"):\n    subprocess.run(\n        [\"git\", \"clone\", \"--depth\", \"1\", \"--filter=blob:none\",\n         \"https://github.com/HarleyCoops/Dakota1890.git\"],\n        check=True, env=env,\n    )\nsys.path.insert(0, \"Dakota1890\")\nprint(\"repo ready\")"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 1. The task data\n\nThe extraction and the lexicon graph live in the repo and in [`HarleyCooper/riggs1890-lexicon-graph`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/HarleyCooper/riggs1890-lexicon-graph). The graph compiles into 68,911 verifiable RL tasks in eight types, plus two evaluation sets with different meanings: `eval_heldout_rule` uses word families training never saw (generalization), and `eval_recall` uses training vocabulary (memory of the volume)."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "from lexgym.recall_curve import fetch_tasks\n\nfetch_tasks(\"HarleyCooper/riggs1890-lexicon-graph\", \"Dakota1890/data/lexgym_tasks\")"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "import json\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nd = Path(\"Dakota1890/data/lexgym_tasks\")\ntrain = [json.loads(l) for l in (d / \"rl_train.jsonl\").open(encoding=\"utf-8\")]\nheld = [json.loads(l) for l in (d / \"eval_heldout_rule.jsonl\").open(encoding=\"utf-8\")]\n\nprint(\"RL train tasks:\", len(train))\nprint(\"by type:\", dict(sorted(Counter(r[\"task\"] for r in train).items())))\nprint(\"held-out (unseen word families):\", len(held))\nprint()\nfor kind in (\"syllabify\", \"pos\", \"gloss\", \"en_to_dak\"):\n    r = next(r for r in train if r[\"task\"] == kind)\n    print(f\"--- {kind} ---\")\n    print(r[\"prompt\"])\n    print(\"gold:\", r[\"answer\"], \"| accepted:\", r[\"accepted\"][:3], \"| page:\", r[\"info\"].get(\"page\"))\n    print()"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "# The lookup tool: what the model may consult during multi-turn tasks.\n# In the real environment this is served automatically up to three times\n# per episode; here we peek at the same index the env serves.\n\nindex = json.loads((d / \"lookup_index.json\").read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\nprint(\"entries in the lookup index:\", len(index))\nsample = \"śuŋka\" if \"śuŋka\" in index else list(index)[0]\nprint(f\"ENTRY for {sample!r}:\")\nprint(\" | \".join(index[sample][:4]))"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 2. The verifiers are the reward\n\nNo LLM judge anywhere. Each task type has one deterministic verifier; only the final answer span (the text after the last `ANSWER:`) is scored, so gold text inside reasoning earns nothing, and every component of a reward goes into a ledger you can reconstruct by hand. The code below is the exact scoring code the training loop uses — [`lexgym/scoring.py`](https://github.com/HarleyCoops/Dakota1890/blob/main/lexgym/scoring.py)."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "from lexgym.scoring import score\n\ne2d = {\"task\": \"en_to_dak\", \"answer\": \"śuŋka\", \"accepted\": [\"śuŋka\"], \"info\": {}}\nsyl = {\"task\": \"syllabify\", \"answer\": \"a-hi'-yo-ka-sin\", \"accepted\": [\"a-hi'-yo-ka-sin\"], \"info\": {}}\n\nprobes = [\n    (e2d, \"ANSWER: śuŋka\",                     \"exact answer\"),\n    (e2d, \"ANSWER: śuŋkata\",                   \"near miss: high character overlap, wrong word\"),\n    (e2d, \"The word for dog is śuŋka.\\nANSWER: mato\", \"gold inside reasoning earns nothing\"),\n    (e2d, \"<think>\\nśuŋka\",                    \"unterminated thinking block: no answer\"),\n    (syl, \"ANSWER: a-hi'-yo-ka-sin\",           \"exact syllable division and accent\"),\n    (syl, \"ANSWER: a-hi-yo'-ka-sin\",           \"right boundaries, wrong stress: partial credit\"),\n    (syl, \"ANSWER: a-hi'-yo-ka-zin\",           \"changed letter: zero\"),\n]\nfor task, completion, label in probes:\n    reward, ledger = score(task, completion)\n    print(f\"{label:48s} reward={reward:5.3f}  span={ledger.get('answer_len', 0):2.0f} chars\")\n\n# The full ledger of one probe: every component, reconstructable by hand.\nprint()\nprint(\"ledger of the partial-credit syllabify answer:\")\nfor k, v in score(syl, \"ANSWER: a-hi-yo'-ka-sin\")[1].items():\n    print(f\"  {k:14s} {v}\")"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 3. Optional: a one-step RL smoke on Tinker\n\nThis runs the real Stage 2 loop for a single step, exactly as the [training jobs](https://huggingface.co/docs/hub/jobs) do, but from the notebook: batch 8 x group 4, one step, ~32 rollouts, well under $0.05 on your Tinker credits. It proves the plumbing end to end. Note the honest caveat from the repo: started from the **base** model the lexical tasks give no gradient, because a model that does not know a Dakota word does not sample one — real RL runs start from the supervised checkpoint. Set `TINKER_API_KEY` (Colab: the key icon, add it as a secret) before running."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "import os\n\nif not os.environ.get(\"TINKER_API_KEY\"):\n    print(\"TINKER_API_KEY not set — skipping the live section (free notebook remains).\")\nelse:\n    %pip install -q tinker==0.39.0 tinker-cookbook==0.6.1 python-dotenv\n    %env WANDB_MODE=offline\n    !cd Dakota1890 && python -m lexgym.train_rl \\\n        --model-name Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B \\\n        --task-dir data/lexgym_tasks \\\n        --log-path colab_rl_smoke \\\n        --batch-size 8 --group-size 4 \\\n        --eval-limit-per-task 2 --eval-every 100 --save-every 100 \\\n        --max-tokens 800 --stage colab-smoke --max-steps 1\n    print(\"done — run folder: Dakota1890/colab_rl_smoke (checkpoints.jsonl holds the Tinker state path)\")"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 4. Inference with the published RL adapter, scored live\n\n[`HarleyCooper/Qwen3.5-4B-Riggs1890-lexgym-pilot`](https://huggingface.co/HarleyCooper/Qwen3.5-4B-Riggs1890-lexgym-pilot) is the pilot model: supervised warm start, then 30 GRPO steps — the stage that took `pos` from 0.38 to 0.73 and restored the lookup behaviour. A free T4 runtime runs the 4B base with the LoRA adapter. We draw tasks from **held-out word families** (families the training rows never saw), generate greedily, and score the model's answers with the same verifiers section 2 used on the hand-written probes.\n\nOne caveat, stated honestly: local generation goes through the tokenizer's chat template, not through Tinker's `disable_thinking` renderer, so these scores are illustrative — the rigorous numbers come from the evaluation jobs in the traces dataset."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "import torch\nprint(\"GPU available:\", torch.cuda.is_available())\n\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\nfrom peft import PeftModel\n\nBASE = \"Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B\"\nADAPTER = \"HarleyCooper/Qwen3.5-4B-Riggs1890-lexgym-pilot\"\n\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(BASE)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(BASE, torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16, device_map=\"auto\")\nmodel = PeftModel.from_pretrained(model, ADAPTER)\nmodel.eval()\nprint(\"model + adapter loaded\")"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "SYSTEM = (\n    \"You work with Stephen Return Riggs' 1890 Dakota-English dictionary. \"\n    \"Answer carefully, then end with one line: 'ANSWER: <your answer>'.\"\n)\n\ndef answer(task):\n    messages = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": SYSTEM}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": task[\"prompt\"]}]\n    try:\n        text = tok.apply_chat_template(messages, tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True,\n                                       enable_thinking=False)\n    except TypeError:  # older templates without the flag\n        text = tok.apply_chat_template(messages, tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True)\n    ids = tok(text, return_tensors=\"pt\").to(model.device)\n    out = model.generate(**ids, max_new_tokens=48, do_sample=False)\n    return tok.decode(out[0][ids.input_ids.shape[1]:], skip_special_tokens=True).strip()\n\nfrom lexgym.scoring import score\n\nprint(f\"{'task':10s} {'reward':>6s}  model answer / gold (held-out families)\")\nprint(\"-\" * 78)\nrewards = {}\nfor kind in (\"syllabify\", \"pos\", \"gloss\", \"en_to_dak\"):\n    t = by[kind][0]\n    completion = answer(t)\n    reward, ledger = score(t, completion)\n    rewards[kind] = reward\n    print(f\"{kind:10s} {reward:6.3f}  {ledger and completion[:60]!r}\")\n    print(f\"{'':10s} {'':6s}  gold: {t['answer']!r}\")\nprint(\"-\" * 78)\nprint(\"mean over the four samples:\", round(sum(rewards.values()) / len(rewards), 3))"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "# The multi-turn lookup behaviour, the thing RL restored after supervised training\n# lost it (0.00 -> 0.85 in the pilot). The environment serves the dictionary entry\n# automatically when the model writes 'LOOKUP: <word>'; here we serve one lookup\n# by hand so the demo needs no rollout loop.\n\nlookup_rows = [r for r in train if r[\"task\"] == \"lookup_gloss\"]\nt = lookup_rows[0]\nprint(\"task prompt:\", t[\"prompt\"][:200], \"...\")\ntarget = t[\"info\"].get(\"target_plain\")\nentry = index.get(target)\nprint(f\"\\n(entry the env would serve for a LOOKUP of {target!r}:)\")\nprint(\"ENTRY:\", \" | \".join(entry[:3]) if entry else \"(not in index)\")\n\nif entry:\n    completion = answer({**t, \"prompt\": t[\"prompt\"] + f\"\\n\\nENTRY: \" + \" | \".join(entry)})\n    reward, ledger = score(t, completion)\n    print(f\"\\nmodel answer with the entry served: {completion[:120]!r}\")\n    print(\"reward:\", round(reward, 3), \"| gold:\", t[\"answer\"])\nelse:\n    print(\"target not in this index copy; pick another row to demo the lookup.\")"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": \"markdown\",
   \"metadata\": {},
   \"source\": \"## Where to go next\\n\\n- **Train it yourself:** [`cookbook/launch_full_rl_pass.py`](https://github.com/HarleyCoops/Dakota1890/blob/main/cookbook/launch_full_rl_pass.py) chains the full RL pass (or one GRPO arm with `--rl-arm`) as Hugging Face Jobs; costs and results are documented in the [README](https://github.com/HarleyCoops/Dakota1890).\\n- **Read the traces:** every rollout of every run — task, conversation, token ids, full reward ledger, advantage — is in [HarleyCooper/riggs1890-lexgym-traces](https://huggingface.co/datasets/HarleyCooper/riggs1890-lexgym-traces).\\n- **Use the environment directly:** the same tasks and scorers are on the Prime Intellect Environments Hub as `harleycooper/riggs1890-lexgym` (`prime env install harleycooper/riggs1890-lexgym`).\\n- **The next experiment:** the [verifier loop](https://github.com/HarleyCoops/Dakota1890#next-the-verifier-loop) compiles Riggs' grammar into an executable checker — RL stays for behaviour, supervised training owns the facts, and Dakota speakers hold the authority over what the book cannot say.\\n\\nA correct score means agreement with one book from 1890. Dakota speakers are the authority on the language.\"\n }\n ]\n}"
